 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.06666666666666667,
   "dur": 3.273,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "Why do we get goosebumps? We hardly have fur."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 4.5,
   "dur": 9.921,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "Nearly every hair has its own tiny muscle, and when we're cold or frightened, a nerve tugs it, pulling the hair upright and the skin into a bump."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 15.233333333333333,
   "dur": 10.709,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "In a furry animal, that fluffs the coat into a blanket of trapped air. We have about as many hair follicles as a chimpanzee, but ours grow fine, almost invisible hairs."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 26.9,
   "dur": 10.946,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "So the bumps look like a leftover. But a team at Harvard found that nerve also reaches the stem cells that grow hair, and the muscle holds it in place."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 40.43333333333333,
   "dur": 9.053,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "In the cold, that nerve wakes the stem cells, and the mice grew new hair sooner. Take the little muscle away, and the wiring fades."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 53.03333333333333,
   "dur": 6.244,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "And most of the hairs it tends are thinner than a third of a sheet of paper."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 60.5,
   "dur": 6.695,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "So why do we still get goosebumps? Because the muscle that raises a hair still looks after it."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 68.43333333333334,
   "dur": 3.696,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "That's the outer layer, for now. Next season, we go deeper."
  }
 ]
}